    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d879">

                <pb facs="0944" n="658" sameAs="frus:frus1916Supp/pg658"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 341.622a/231</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        German Ambassador</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Bernstorff</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 2122</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-09-26">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">September 26, 1916</hi>
                        </date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Excellency</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the
                    receipt of your note of September 20, 1916,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d879-f1"/> in
                    which, with reference to previous <pb facs="0945" n="659" xml:id="pg659"/>
                    correspondence concerning the Germans taken from the American S. S. <hi
                        rendition="#italic">China</hi> by the British authorities, you request that
                    instructions be sent to the American Consul General at Sydney to send these
                    persons directly to San Francisco, and not to Shanghai first, if the
                    negotiations for their release are brought to a successful issue. You add that
                    the roundabout route to Shanghai would only entail unnecessary expense to those
                    concerned.</p>
                <p>In reply I have the honor to say that these negotiations have ended in an
                    arrangement for the transportation of these persons back to Shanghai, and that
                    this Government deems it inexpedient further to delay their departure, which is
                    to take place on September 28, by negotiating for their transportation to San
                    Francisco.</p>
                <p>Accept [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Robert Lansing</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
